/-
# Subformations of the formation generated by a finite group (Kourovka Notebook Problem 9.59)

Kourovka Notebook, Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 9.59 (A. N. Skiba and L. A. Shemetkov (after
W. Gaschütz)): prove that the formation generated by a finite group has finite lattice of
subformations.
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import WantedExt.GroupTheory.FiniteGroupFormationShared

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.GeneratedFormationSubformationsKourovka9Problem59Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 9.59 (A. N.
Skiba and L. A. Shemetkov). UnsolvedMath record UM-00700592 (`KOU-9.59`), dataset
ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

Formations are `FiniteGroupClass` predicates with `IsFormation` from the 13.51 entry; the formation
generated by `G` is the intersection of all formations containing `G`. Formations are closed under
homomorphic images, hence under isomorphisms, so distinct subformations are distinct predicates and
"finite lattice of subformations" is finiteness of the set of formations contained in the generated
formation (allowing the empty formation changes the count by one).
-/

open MathlibExt.GroupTheory.FiniteModularLatticeFormationsKourovka13Problem51Wanted
open MathlibExt.GroupTheory.FiniteGroupFormationShared


/-- The formation generated by a finite group `G`: the intersection of all formations containing
`G`. -/
def formationGeneratedBy (G : Type) [Group G] [Finite G] : FiniteGroupClass :=
  fun H _ _ => ∀ 𝔉 : FiniteGroupClass, IsFormation 𝔉 → 𝔉 G → 𝔉 H

/-- Kourovka 9.59 (A. N. Skiba, L. A. Shemetkov, after W. Gaschütz): the formation generated by a
finite group has only finitely many subformations (its lattice of subformations is finite). -/
def generatedFormationFinitelyManySubformations : Prop :=
  ∀ (G : Type) [Group G] [Finite G],
    {𝔊 : FiniteGroupClass | IsFormation 𝔊 ∧ ClassLE 𝔊 (formationGeneratedBy G)}.Finite

/--
Resolved false: the Kourovka Archive records that counterexamples are constructed by Burichenko (On
a problem of Gaschütz): some finite group generates a formation with infinitely many subformations;
the counterexamples are groups of type `2S₅` (zbMATH review Zbl 1280.20017). Source: E. I. Khukhro
and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue,
arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 9.59 (A. N. Skiba and L. A.
Shemetkov); resolution: V. P. Burichenko, On a problem of Gaschütz, J. Algebra 372 (2012), 428-458,
zbMATH review Zbl 1280.20017: the answer to Gaschütz's problem is negative, the counterexamples
being groups of type 2S₅; Kourovka Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 9.59: Counterexamples are
constructed, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jalgebra.2012.10.006.
-/
public theorem_wanted generatedFormationFinitelyManySubformations_refuted :
    ¬ generatedFormationFinitelyManySubformations

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.GeneratedFormationSubformationsKourovka9Problem59Wanted
